    <!-- 05 / WHAT INDIA IMPORTS TODAY -->
    <section id="import-dependence" class="scroll-mt-24 space-y-6">
      <div class="section-rule">
        <h2 class="section-heading">05 / What India Imports Today</h2>
        <span class="section-meta">Ministry of Mines &bull; CSEP &bull; GTRI &bull; IMTMA</span>
      </div>

      <div class="text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 leading-relaxed text-sm sm:text-base space-y-4 max-w-3xl">
        <p>
          Rising incomes tell you what Indian households will buy more of. Trade data tells you where our factories and infrastructure are still fragile.
        </p>
        <p>
          Over the last decade, India has set up assembly lines for phones, EVs, solar panels, and medicines. That is real progress. Still, if you look one layer deeper at the metals, chemicals, magnets, and machines that feed those factories, India imports almost everything from one or two countries.
        </p>
        <p>
          For years, buying from the cheapest global supplier worked fine. Today, countries use export controls on minerals, magnets, and factory tools as leverage. If access is tightened tomorrow, several large sectors in India will struggle to keep running.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Table: Verified Import Reliance Across Critical Inputs -->
      <figure class="data-card">
        <div class="card-header">
          <div>
            <h3 class="card-title">Critical Imports in India's Supply Chain</h3>
            <p class="card-subtitle">Share of domestic demand met by imports &bull; Source: Ministry of Mines, CSEP, GTRI &amp; IMTMA</p>
          </div>
          <span class="card-badge">60% to 100% Imported</span>
        </div>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Material / Input</th>
                <th class="num">Import Share</th>
                <th>Main Suppliers</th>
                <th>What Stops If Cut Off</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="row-accent">
                <th scope="row">Lithium, Cobalt &amp; Nickel</th>
                <td class="num font-semibold">100%</td>
                <td>China (refining), Chile, Australia</td>
                <td>EV batteries, grid storage, electronics</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-accent">
                <th scope="row">Rare-Earth Magnets (NdFeB)</th>
                <td class="num font-semibold">&gt;90%</td>
                <td>China (~90% global processing)</td>
                <td>EV traction motors, wind turbines, drones</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-accent">
                <th scope="row">Solar Wafers &amp; Polysilicon</th>
                <td class="num font-semibold">&gt;90%</td>
                <td>China</td>
                <td>Domestic solar cell and panel plants</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-accent">
                <th scope="row">CRGO Electrical Steel</th>
                <td class="num font-semibold">~90%</td>
                <td>China, Japan, South Korea, Russia</td>
                <td>Power grid and distribution transformers</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Potash &amp; Phosphate Inputs</th>
                <td class="num font-semibold">85-100%</td>
                <td>Canada, Russia, Jordan, Morocco</td>
                <td>Fertilizers and farm productivity</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Pharma KSMs &amp; Bulk APIs</th>
                <td class="num font-semibold">65-70%</td>
                <td>China (80-90% in antibiotics)</td>
                <td>Basic medicines, antibiotics, paracetamol</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">High-Precision CNC Machines</th>
                <td class="num font-semibold">60-65%</td>
                <td>Germany, Japan, Taiwan, China</td>
                <td>Auto, aerospace, and electronics factories</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p class="card-footnote">
          Take power transformers as one example. India needs about 4.0 to 4.5 lakh tonnes of cold-rolled grain-oriented (CRGO) steel every year to build transformer cores. Domestic mills produce less than 50,000 tonnes. The rest has to be imported.
        </p>
      </figure>

      <!-- Cambrian-style Elasticity / Hold-Up Asymmetry Panels -->
      <div class="text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 leading-relaxed text-sm sm:text-base space-y-3 max-w-3xl pt-2">
        <h3 class="font-serif text-lg sm:text-xl font-bold text-stone-950 dark:text-stone-50">
          Small input, big hold-up
        </h3>
        <p>
          Why do these bottlenecks matter so much to a business owner? Often, the imported part is only a small fraction of the final product's cost. Still, without it, the entire assembly line or infrastructure project sits idle. You cannot ship a ₹12 lakh electric car if you are missing ₹3,500 worth of magnets, and you cannot turn on a ₹500 crore solar park without a ₹4 crore transformer.
        </p>
        <p>
          That cost asymmetry makes downstream buyers price-inelastic. A factory owner will gladly pay a fair price for a reliable domestic alternative rather than risk a line shutdown.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="el-grid">
        <!-- Panel 1: Component Cost Share vs Line-Stop Risk -->
        <div class="data-card">
          <div class="el-label">Component Cost Asymmetry</div>
          <div class="stat-hero text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark mt-1">2% to 25%</div>
          <p class="el-sub">of total product cost, yet 100% line-stopping if supply is cut</p>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="el-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Imported Input</th>
                  <th scope="col">% of Cost</th>
                  <th scope="col">Imported</th>
